From d2b56463c55c5d5e6fd72ce23409286cc164d92a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 01:09:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] media: rkisp1: Add a MIPI additional-data capture video node

The MIPI receiver can divert CSI-2 long packets whose (VC, DT) matches
one of its four additional-data selectors into a small FIFO, entirely
separate from the image path. This is the only out-of-band data path
this ISP generation has - later Rockchip ISPs grew a DMA engine for it.
The IMX258 delivers its shield-pixel (PDAF) samples as such packets,
with data type 0x2f, during line blanking. The driver defines the
registers and unmasks the overflow interrupt, but never programs the
selectors, so the FIFO stays unused.

Add a rkisp1_addata metadata capture node that delivers one buffer per
frame: a small header (flags, packet count, byte counts) followed by the
verbatim FIFO content. Hardware probing on the PinePhone Pro established
the FIFO framing - each packet appears as its 4-byte MIPI long-packet
header followed by the dword-padded payload - and the kernel walks those
headers only to end the buffer on a whole packet and to detect desync;
the payload semantics stay in userspace, because the shield-pixel layout
is undocumented and still being reverse engineered.

The same probing measured the FIFO at 2048 dwords, which a sensor can
overrun well within one frame, so the capture is drained by a 1 ms timer
while active, plus a final drain at frame end. The hardware's own
fill-level interrupt is deliberately not used: neither mainline nor the
vendor driver has ever unmasked it, its clear semantics are undocumented,
and on RK3399 - where the whole ISP shares one interrupt line - unmasking
it hung the machine as soon as matching data arrived. There is no DMA on
this path, so every drain is a bounded loop of register reads in hard
interrupt context; the per-frame cost scales with how much data the
sensor is configured to emit, which is userspace's choice, as is the
per-frame capture limit (fmt.meta.buffersize, clamped to 8 KB - 1 MB).
The actual amount captured each frame is variable and reported in
bytesused and in the header.

The selectors are programmed from the sensor's get_frame_desc() - every
CSI-2 entry whose data type differs from the image stream's - and only
while the capture node is streaming; otherwise they are parked at their
match-nothing reset value so the FIFO stays empty. An add_data_dt module
parameter overrides the frame descriptor for experiments (it also serves
sensors that do not implement get_frame_desc() yet).

The additional-data overflow interrupt is excluded from the mipi_error
accounting, which used to count any status other than exactly FRAME_END
as an error. Debugfs gains capture counters and a MIPI register dump
(minus the FIFO register, since reading it pops data); CUR_DATA_ID in
that dump is the cheapest way to see whether a sensor's out-of-band
packets reach the receiver at all.

Signed-off-by: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
